"""Refresh one existing purchase comparison; never regenerate the library or homepage."""
import re
import expand_hold_period as page

page.SLUG='airbnb-vs-long-term-rentals'
page.TITLE='Airbnb vs Long-Term Rentals: Compare Before Buying'
page.H1='Airbnb vs Long-Term Rentals: Which Actually Makes More Money?'
page.DESC='Compare Airbnb and long-term rental purchases using lawful use, quoted costs, setup cash and a worked downside—not gross revenue or a promised tax benefit.'
page.FAQ=[
('Do short-term rentals make more money than long-term rentals?', 'Not necessarily. Compare supported collected revenue, owner-paid costs, debt, setup cash and replacement reserves for the same property. Higher gross receipts can coexist with worse cash flow.'),
('Are short-term rentals riskier than long-term rentals?', 'The exposures differ. Review legal use, permission changes, seasonality, collection, possession, insurance and operating responsibilities for each strategy. Neither guarantees timely payment or lawful vacant possession.'),
('Why might a high-income buyer consider an STR?', 'The property may fit the buyer’s acquisition objectives, but income and a large tax bill do not establish suitability. Have a CPA separately assess activity classification, participation and loss limitations without funding the purchase from an assumed refund.'),
('Can a long-term lease rescue an unsuccessful STR purchase?', 'Only if the actual long-term use is lawful and its separate rent, expenses, conversion costs and timing support the fallback. Do not substitute a percentage of STR revenue for local lease evidence.')]
page.BODY='''<p class="lead">An Airbnb-versus-long-term-rental comparison should answer whether you should buy the property, not which strategy has the more impressive revenue headline. Compare the same address under two lawful uses, with actual buyer financing, supported receipts, owner-paid costs and setup cash. A higher-income buyer also needs a separate adviser-reviewed tax assessment, but a large tax bill does not make weak property economics acceptable. Neither strategy automatically makes more money.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring a candidate property and both rental scenarios to an acquisition call</a>. This guide is for a purchase within the next zero to six months. It does not recommend an STR, a long-term lease or a tax position without the property's evidence and the buyer's constraints.</p>
<h2>Start with rights and evidence, not a revenue multiple</h2>
<p>Confirm the new owner's intended STR permission, applicable licensing, covenants and insurer acceptance. Separately establish whether the proposed long-term lease is allowed and feasible. Do not assume losing short-stay permission leaves an immediately executable lease fallback. Existing tenants, repair work, advertising time and contractual obligations can delay a change of use. The <a href="/blog/str-long-term-rental-fallback/">long-term fallback checklist</a> starts that separate review.</p>
<p>For the STR case, reconcile seller receipts to the purchased property and support comparable revenue, available nights and seasonality. For the lease case, request comparable executed or supportable lease terms, vacancy and collection assumptions, concessions and responsibility for utilities. Asking rent is not collected rent. A strong holiday week is not an annual STR forecast. Remove owner-blocked dates and unavailable rooms before comparing either plan.</p>
<p>Ask the lender and insurer to confirm the actual use and terms for each alternative. Holding debt constant is useful for a controlled worksheet, but the final decision must reflect any real differences in eligibility, loan payment or coverage. Do not represent an investment STR as a second home to improve the quote. Set the evidence deadline while usable contract protections remain.</p>
<h2>Build a comparison register with nonoverlapping costs</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Purchase question</th><th>Evidence for each lawful use</th><th>What changes the decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Supported receipts</td><td>Property-specific rental records/comparables; vacancy and collection assumptions</td><td>Replace seller headline totals with a defensible annual case</td></tr>
<tr><td>Owner-paid expenses</td><td>Management, utilities, cleaning, taxes, insurance, repairs and fee quotes</td><td>Separate tenant/guest reimbursement from the matching expense</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debt and closing</td><td>Actual loan payment, cash to close and use acceptance</td><td>A qualifying loan does not establish profitable operation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Setup and conversion</td><td>Furnishing, repairs, lease-up, permits and alternative-use work</td><td>Identify money due before income can arrive</td></tr>
<tr><td>Replacement and retained cash</td><td>Property-condition schedule and buyer-specific reserve target</td><td>A reserve allocation is not an operating expense or a promised repair budget</td></tr>
<tr><td>Owner responsibilities</td><td>Written management scope, exclusions and escalation coverage</td><td>Unassigned work remains the owner's problem, not a free service</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Record amounts, evidence dates, responsible reviewers and unresolved items. A management percentage may apply to a different revenue base than your worksheet assumes; establish what it covers and which services cost extra. Use the prospective operator's arrangement, not a universal expense band or workload-hour promise. Keep capital work and ordinary repairs distinct and do not deduct a replacement allowance twice.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/1857">Airbnb's current service-fee guidance</a> describes split and single fee structures and an ongoing migration away from split fees. It states most single-fee hosts pay15.5%, with exceptions. That is not a universal fee for every reservation or platform. Verify the actual prospective listing/manager treatment and the fee base, then include it once in the STR cost case. A seller's historical3% assumption may not describe your new operation.</p>
<h2>Worked same-property cash comparison</h2>
<p>All numbers below are hypothetical decision inputs, not a market forecast, client result or loan quote. Collected receipts are shown before the owner's modeled expense stack. The cost totals include owner-paid property tax and insurance, management and other recurring costs as applicable; they exclude debt, one-time setup and the distinct replacement allocations shown below. Both base cases use the same illustrative$30,000 annual debt payment to isolate the operating comparison.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Annual illustrative line</th><th>Long-term lease</th><th>STR base</th><th>STR downside</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Collected receipts</td><td>$36,000</td><td>$72,000</td><td>$57,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nondebt owner costs</td><td>$12,000</td><td>$40,000</td><td>$37,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Before-debt operating balance</td><td>$24,000</td><td>$32,000</td><td>$20,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debt payment</td><td>$30,000</td><td>$30,000</td><td>$30,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cash before replacement allocation</td><td>−$6,000</td><td>$2,000</td><td>−$10,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Distinct retained replacement allocation</td><td>$2,000</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$5,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>After allocation</td><td>−$8,000</td><td>−$3,000</td><td>−$15,000</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The STR base produces twice the collected revenue but only$8,000 more before debt. It has$2,000 cash after debt and before the retained allocation, not$72,000 of profit. Allocating$5,000 to future replacements creates a$3,000 funding requirement beyond current operating cash. The allocation remains cash if retained; it is not a repair already incurred. Do not call the debt-inclusive or allocation-adjusted balance NOI.</p>
<p>The downside reduces receipts by$15,000 while only$3,000 of costs disappear. The remaining$37,000 includes fixed and continuing obligations; lower bookings do not eliminate every bill. The resulting$15,000 after-allocation funding requirement must fit independently available capital. Neither base case passes a buyer requiring nonnegative annual cash after the stated allocations. An STR's relative advantage does not turn an unacceptable absolute result into a suitable purchase.</p>
<p>Now compare early cash. Suppose common purchase/closing funds total$130,000, while STR setup requires$35,000 and long-term preparation$8,000. Before retained reserves, commitments are$165,000 and$138,000, a$27,000 difference. Add the actual reserve target and separately dated carry costs. Do not add setup to the annual operating expense table or count a deposit again if credited at closing. Use the <a href="/tools/first-str-purchase-budget/">purchase budget</a> to test all-in funds.</p>
<h2>Evaluate tax suitability separately from purchase cash</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p925">IRS Publication925</a> distinguishes rental-activity exceptions from material participation and distinguishes active from material participation. The seven-day average-use exception is not an automatic salary offset. The publication also addresses basis, at-risk and passive-loss restrictions; qualified advisers must evaluate applicable limitations and your ownership/use facts. Its currently published edition is2025, checked October7,2026.</p>
<p>Ask the CPA to compare usable deductions and changes in tax payable under each actual strategy, including participation, personal use and disposition. Do not multiply an unsupported depreciation estimate by a marginal rate and call the result a refund. Long-term treatment is not universally a zero benefit, and short-term treatment is not universally an immediate benefit. See the <a href="/blog/str-tax-benefit-shortfall-purchase/">smaller, delayed or unavailable benefit test</a>. Closing and launch must work without speculative tax receipts.</p>
<h2>Choose a purchase that survives its real responsibilities</h2>
<p>Neither a lease nor a short reservation guarantees collection or timely possession. Ask local counsel about applicable occupancy and remedies, and ask the insurer about the intended operation. Request management response coverage, emergency responsibilities and service exclusions. Replace generic hours-per-month promises with a documented responsibility plan. Hiring help does not eliminate every owner task or determine tax participation.</p>
<p>Proceed when lawful use, supported receipts, quoted costs, early cash and a meaningful downside meet the buyer's actual requirements. Reprice a property when verified costs change an otherwise viable plan; do not assume a lender will accept the new structure. Delay within valid protections while decisive evidence is missing. Decline when both strategies fail or the fallback depends on unverified permissions, instant lease-up or an assumed refund.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Discuss the property with the completed two-strategy register</a>. BNB Accelerator assists within its acquisition scope; independent professionals determine legal, tax, lending and insurance conclusions. Keep financial records in secure agreed channels. Primary sources reviewed October7,2026. Educational information only, not personalized investment, tax, legal or lending advice. No permission, financing, tenant payment, booking, deduction, refund or return is guaranteed.</p>'''

if __name__=='__main__':
 page.BODY=''.join(part if part.startswith('<') else re.sub(r'(?<=[A-Za-z])(?=[\d$])',' ',part).replace('7,2026','7, 2026') for part in re.split(r'(<[^>]+>)',page.BODY))
 page.main('2026-08-10','data-str-ltr-comparison-review-link','Compare the <a href="/blog/airbnb-vs-long-term-rentals/">same-property STR and long-term cash worksheet</a> before treating higher gross receipts or a projected tax benefit as a purchase decision.')
